@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "Dakar 2002";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

	<div class="text-center">
		<h1>
			Summary (2002 - Aras - Dakar)
		</h1>
		<p>
			<img src="2002_map.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			<img src="2002_01.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			Although the Mitsubishis were traded as hot favourites in 2002, the overwhelming
			victory was anything but certain. Tough competition was expected from the Renault
			Kangoo buggies, with Jean-Louis Schlesser and Jose Maria Servia at the wheel. Nissan,
			too, with Stephane Peterhansel and Gregoire De Mevius, was considered a hard opponent.
			And so it came to pass that De Mevius found himself in first place before the rally
			crossed over to Africa. But once in Africa, Masuoka instantly grabbed
			the lead and did not give it up again until the finish. The threat of the Schlesser buggies
			never became reality, as they had to battle engine problems early on.
			<img src="2002_02.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			The danger to Mitsubishi's victory manifested itself more and more in De Mevius, who with
			his Terrano swept through the first half of the rally in third place, close behind the
			Mitsubishis. But in Zouerat he too was struck by engine problems and could
			<img src="2002_03.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			no longer keep up with the pace of the Mitsubishis. From that moment on it was certain
			that a Mitsubishi would win the rally. The only question was: which one? Mitsubishi
			clearly stated for the record that there would be no team orders, and so a battle unfolded
			between Masuoka, Kleinschmidt and Shinozuka for the overall victory. On the circuit
			of Atar, Kleinschmidt and Shinozuka fought a thrilling head-to-head race,
			in which each of the two contenders was plagued by small mishaps. In the end,
			Jutta Kleinschmidt emerged as the winner after Shinozuka rammed a tree
			and suffered a puncture. Masuoka extended his lead further and caused
			<img src="2002_04.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			a moment of shock when he jumped over a sand dune side by side with Jean-Pierre Fontenay
			and the two Pajeros came within a hair's breadth of touching. But
			this incident ended without harm, and at the end of the stage Masuoka had extended his lead
			over second place to an almost unassailable 52 minutes. Actually,
			with a 52-minute lead it should be easy to win a race, but this
			was the Dakar, and as everyone knows a lot can happen there before all four tyres
			<img src="2002_05.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			have rolled over the finish line. And on the route between Tichit and Kiffa, the
			actually impossible then happened. A heavy storm and rainfall turned the route
			into a mudslide. Even the most experienced Dakar veterans had never seen
			a picture and a route like the one the drivers were now facing. In poor visibility
			and difficult terrain, almost the entire field missed a badly marked
			waypoint from the roadbook and got lost. Kleinschmidt, however, navigated perfectly
			<img src="2002_06.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			and made up 20 minutes on Shinozuka. Despite this magnificent performance, it was
			no longer possible for Kleinschmidt to make up the remaining time on Masuoka on the short
			circuit around Dakar, and so a beaming Masuoka could be seen on the finish ramp
			in Dakar, having won a truly hard and unusual rally.
		</p>
